Here is an example of how you can create a `ContentHouse` component in Vue.js using TypeScript, with the ability to fetch data from a remote API and display it in a list format:
```
import axios from 'axios';
import { defineComponent, ref, onMounted } from 'vue';

interface ParsedContent {
  id: number;
  title: string;
  description: string;
}

const ContentHouse = defineComponent({
  name: 'ContentHouse',
  setup() {
    const contents = ref([]);

    onMounted(() => {
      getContents();
    });

    function getContents() {
      return axios.get('https://example.com/api/contents')
        .then((response) => {
          contents.value = response.data;
        })
        .catch((error) => {
          console.log(error);
        });
    }

    return {
      contents,
      getContents,
    };
  },
});

export default ContentHouse;
```
This component uses the `defineComponent` method from Vue.js to define a reusable component called `ContentHouse`. The `setup()` method is used to initialize the component's state and methods. In this case, we are defining two variables: `contents`, which will hold the array of content objects retrieved from the API, and `getContents()`, which is a method that makes an HTTP GET request to retrieve the contents from the API.

The `onMounted()` hook is used to call the `getContents()` method when the component is mounted, so that the contents are loaded immediately after the component is rendered.

To render the contents in a list format, you can use the following template:
```
<template>
  <div class="content-house">
    <ul v-if="contents && contents.length > 0" :key="item.id" v-for="item in contents">
      <li>{{ item.title }} - {{ item.description }}</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</template>
```
In this template, we are using the `v-if` directive to check if there are any contents available before rendering them. If there are no contents, nothing will be displayed. Otherwise, we loop through each content object using the `v-for` directive and display its title and description in a list item (`<li>` element).

Note that this is just one possible implementation of a `ContentHouse` component, and you may need to modify it depending on your specific requirements.
